  "cues": [
   [
    0.117,
    "With oxygen present, pyruvate leaves the cytosol and enters the mitochondrion, through a transport protein."
   ],
   [
    7.357,
    "Remember what it is: a chain of three carbons."
   ],
   [
    10.443,
    "Before the cycle can begin, pyruvate must be converted to acetyl co-A, which links the cycle to glycolysis."
   ],
   [
    17.891,
    "The enzyme is pyruvate dehydrogenase, a big complex of many subunits, and it works in three steps."
   ],
   [
    25.299,
    "Step one."
   ],
   [
    26.263,
    "The carboxyl carbon, one of the three, is cut off and leaves as carbon dioxide."
   ],
   [
    31.81,
    "Step two."
   ],
   [
    32.786,
    "The two carbons that remain are oxidized."
   ],
   [
    35.586,
    "Their electrons go to N A D plus, which becomes N A D H."
   ],
   [
    40.781,
    "Step three."
   ],
   [
    41.94,
    "Coenzyme A, a carrier molecule, attaches to what is left."
   ],
   [
    46.719,
    "That is acetyl co-A: two carbons, the acetyl group, riding on coenzyme A."
   ],
   [
    52.773,
    "Count the carbons."
   ],
   [
    54.167,
    "Three went in."
   ],
   [
    55.13,
    "One left as carbon dioxide, and two carry on."
   ],
   [
    58.607,
    "Some books call this the link reaction, because it links glycolysis to the cycle."
   ]
  ]
 },
